    "failure_scenario": "Rule: \"Standard formatters: `ruff format`, `ruff check --fix`, `mypy --strict`.\" Breaking line: `import pandas as pd`. Running the project virtual environment's mypy with the governing `[tool.mypy] strict = true` reports `[import-untyped]` here and exits 1, so the required type-check gate cannot validate the source and tests until `pandas-stubs` is declared or the import is handled consistently."
  },
  {
    "file": "/Users/doob/dev/energy_forecast_ws/dbc151/project/runs/dbc151/forecast_load.py",
    "line": 50,
    "summary": "Backtest metrics use a loose dictionary even though the metric schema is fixed.",
    "failure_scenario": "Rule: \"Prefer dataclasses or `pydantic.BaseModel` over loose dicts when the shape is fixed.\" Breaking line: `metrics: dict[str, float]`. The code always expects named fields such as `mae_mw`, `mape_percent`, and `r_squared`, but `dict[str, float]` cannot require them. A misspelled or omitted metric passes static typing and fails later as a `KeyError` or silently changes the emitted report schema."
  },
  {
    "file": "/Users/doob/dev/energy_forecast_ws/dbc151/project/runs/dbc151/forecast_load.py",
    "line": 406,
    "summary": "The fixed metrics-report schema is assembled as an untyped nested dictionary.",
    "failure_scenario": "Rules: \"Type hints on every function signature and on non-trivial locals\" and \"Prefer dataclasses or `pydantic.BaseModel` over loose dicts when the shape is fixed.\" Breaking line: `report = {`. This non-trivial local defines the complete JSON contract without a type or validating model, so a renamed field, wrong nested value type, or omitted section is accepted and written to disk, creating undetected output-schema drift for downstream consumers."
  }
]

